# Cloudflare Pages redirects. Two things about this file decide whether a rule
# actually fires, and both are counter-intuitive:
#
#  1. ORDER, NOT SPECIFICITY. "If there are multiple redirects for the same
#     source path, the top-most redirect is applied." A general wildcard placed
#     above a specific rule therefore SWALLOWS it. That is why the ai-jargon
#     alias below sits above /posts/*, not below it.
#  2. A SLASH-LESS PATH IS ITS OWN SOURCE. /x/* does not match /x, and this site
#     has no custom 404 to catch the difference, so every moved path gets a bare
#     rule as well as a wildcard.
#
# Every rule is 301 (permanent): 302 tells search engines the move is temporary
# and the old URL keeps the ranking. No rule's target is another rule's source
# (no redirect chains) - asserted by scripts/test_redirects_order.py.

# blog-priv#59: Consulting -> Hire Hoi URL migration. The old /consulting/* tree
# now lives at /hire-hoi/ai-consultancy/*. Explicit 301 (permanent) so search
# engines and externally cached share-cards follow the move.
# The bare /consulting (no trailing slash) needs its own rule: the wildcard below
# does not match a slash-less path, and there is no custom 404 to catch it.
/consulting /hire-hoi/ai-consultancy/ 301
/consulting/* /hire-hoi/ai-consultancy/:splat 301

# blog-priv#62: "Blogs" became a real level in the information architecture, so
# the 79 posts and the 7 category landings moved under /blogs/. 87 retired URLs,
# each mapping 1:1 (scripts/test_redirects_coverage.py asserts every one of them
# resolves to a URL the new sitemap actually serves).
#
# These 39 files across four other repos link to the retired URLs - including a
# submitted funding application and material already sent to recruiters - and
# cannot be retroactively edited. The rules below are the only thing keeping
# those links alive.

# The one post whose published path is not its slug: content/posts/ai-jargon-for-noobs/
# sets `slug: ai-jargon-for-newbies`, so the /posts/* wildcard would send it to a
# dead /blogs/ai-jargon-for-noobs/. It MUST stay above that wildcard (see 1 above).
/posts/ai-jargon-for-noobs /blogs/ai-jargon-for-newbies/ 301
/posts/ai-jargon-for-noobs/ /blogs/ai-jargon-for-newbies/ 301

# Posts. The wildcard also carries the section feed (/posts/index.xml ->
# /blogs/index.xml), since :splat is the whole remainder of the path.
/posts /blogs/ 301
/posts/* /blogs/:splat 301

# The 7 category landings. Each wildcard also carries that category's RSS feed
# (/tech-ai/index.xml -> /blogs/tech-ai/index.xml). Category order matches
# config/_default/menus.toml.
/tech-ai /blogs/tech-ai/ 301
/tech-ai/* /blogs/tech-ai/:splat 301
/entrepreneurship /blogs/entrepreneurship/ 301
/entrepreneurship/* /blogs/entrepreneurship/:splat 301
/trading /blogs/trading/ 301
/trading/* /blogs/trading/:splat 301
/food-booze /blogs/food-booze/ 301
/food-booze/* /blogs/food-booze/:splat 301
/adventure /blogs/adventure/ 301
/adventure/* /blogs/adventure/:splat 301
/dance /blogs/dance/ 301
/dance/* /blogs/dance/:splat 301
/life /blogs/life/ 301
/life/* /blogs/life/:splat 301

# The `categories` taxonomy is switched off (blog-priv#62 Phase 6, hugo.toml
# [taxonomies]). Its 8 URLs listed exactly the same posts as the landings above,
# so each term maps onto its landing and the index maps onto the hub. `tags` and
# `series` are deliberately untouched (A11) and have no rules here.
/categories /blogs/ 301
/categories/* /blogs/:splat 301
